

METHODS

Overview

The Methods section describes the computational, mathematical, and astrophysical procedures used to implement the BLACKWELL‑01 architecture. It details:

data ingestion

invariant computation

entropy harvesting

invariant synchronization

encryption substrate generation

simulation output production

Each subsection is designed to be independently verifiable by external institutions.

1. Astrophysical Dataset Acquisition

Astrophysical data is sourced from two primary repositories:

Gaia DR3 stellar proper motion vectors

NASA Exoplanet Archive transit timing variations (TTVs)

Data is pulled at fixed epoch intervals using the Epoch Ingestion Protocol.

Processing steps:

Convert RA/Dec + proper motion into velocity vectors

Normalize TTV curves into ΔL (luminosity deviation) sequences

Quantize both into bit‑level entropy packets

Store packets in the Entropy Harvesting Module (EHM)

This ensures that all downstream invariants operate on real cosmic motion, not synthetic noise.

2. Entropy Harvesting Module (EHM)

The EHM transforms astrophysical motion into structured entropy.

2.1 Quantization Procedure

Velocity vectors and TTV curves are discretized using:

[ Q(x) = \lfloor x \cdot \beta \rfloor ]

where β is the quantization constant tuned to stellar motion variance.

2.2 Entropy Extraction

Entropy is computed via:

[ S = \sum_{i=1}^{N} (1 - |v_i - \bar{v}|) ]

This produces an entropy pool that feeds EMI.

3. Computation of EMI (Entropy‑Motion Invariant)

EMI is computed using the rule engine:

[ \text{EMI} = (\psi(\mu) \otimes \phi(\lambda)) \oplus \left(\frac{\Delta E}{\Delta t}\right) ]

3.1 ψ(μ): Bits → Gravitational Potential

ψ maps quantized bits into gravitational potential perturbations using:

[ \psi(\mu) = G \cdot \frac{\mu}{r^2} ]

3.2 φ(λ): Motion → Structured Field

φ maps stellar motion into a structured field tensor.

3.3 Energy Flow Term

[ \frac{\Delta E}{\Delta t} ] is derived from luminosity drift and motion variance.

This produces the entropy‑motion invariant substrate.

4. Computation of MEDI (Mass‑Energy Decompress Invariant)

MEDI is computed via:

[ \text{MEDI} = (\Phi(\mu) \otimes \Lambda(\lambda)) \oplus \left(\frac{\Delta M}{\Delta t}\right) ]

4.1 Φ(μ): Gravitational Potential → Bits

Inverse mapping of ψ, converting gravitational potential back into bit‑level structure.

4.2 Λ(λ): Structural Configuration → Field

Λ maps cosmic structure stability (CMB fluctuations, halo density profiles) into a field tensor.

4.3 Mass Drift Term

[ \frac{\Delta M}{\Delta t} ] is computed from halo density evolution and baryonic mass drift.

This produces the mass‑energy decompress invariant substrate.

5. Integrated Invariant Processor (IIP)

The IIP synchronizes EMI and MEDI.

5.1 Synchronization Bus

A dual‑channel bus aligns:

entropy‑motion gradients

mass‑energy decompress gradients

using:

[ \Gamma = \alpha \cdot \text{EMI} + \beta \cdot \text{MEDI} ]

5.2 Invariant Substrate Output

The output is a unified invariant tensor used for encryption and simulation.

6. RADE: Real‑Time Astrophysics Dataset Encryption

RADE uses the invariant substrate to generate evolving cryptographic keys.

6.1 HKDF Key Derivation

HKDF extracts key material from Γ:

[ K = \text{HKDF}(\Gamma) ]

6.2 AES‑256‑GCM Encryption

Data is encrypted using:

[ C = \text{AES}_{256\text{-GCM}}(D, K) ]

6.3 Key Evolution Cycle

Keys evolve according to:

[ K_{t+1} = K_t \oplus \left(\frac{\Delta \text{EMI}}{\Delta t} E_0\right) ]

This produces moving‑target encryption synchronized to cosmic motion.

